I’ll first inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the provided diffraction workflow documentation. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, build and refine a multiphase GSAS-II model, and verify that every submitted artifact is generated from the final project.

The chemistry narrows the search strongly to the Sr–W–O system, but the 650 °C treatment makes residual precursor and intermediate phases plausible. I’m now extracting the exact output requirements and the frozen-library access paths before assigning peaks.

The major phase is unambiguous: its full peak sequence is the scheelite structure of SrWO₄. Two weak peak families align with monoclinic WO₃ and orthorhombic SrCO₃ after the same specimen/zero displacement, consistent with an incomplete 1:1 precursor reaction. I’m testing those three assignments against all relevant library polymorphs before refinement.

The initial three-phase model is now in GSAS-II. I’m refining in conservative stages—background and scales, then cells/displacement, then line broadening—so I can catch parameter correlations rather than letting a single unconstrained fit absorb the weak phases.

The unconstrained fit exposed a classic GSAS-II trap: size/strain terms wandered outside physical limits, and the saved tree was then clipped, so its stored curve no longer replayed. I’ve rejected that numerical minimum and am iterating from bounded, physically meaningful values; replay consistency is being checked after each refinement.

The bounded replay now fits at Rwp ≈ 4.39%, only modestly above the Poisson floor of 3.98%. The refined fractions are stabilizing near 83% SrWO₄, 11% WO₃, and 6% SrCO₃. I’m checking the remaining small residuals for phase-specific texture rather than treating them as extra phases.

The three-phase model is now stable at essentially the counting-statistics limit: scheelite SrWO₄ with minor monoclinic WO₃ and orthorhombic SrCO₃. I’m doing the final replay-consistency pass now—freezing the model, exporting directly from that GSAS-II project, and checking the CIFs, fractions, and profile against one another.

